---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/docs/guides/mcp-setup.html', label: 'MCP Server Setup guide' },
  { href: '/questions/how-do-i-connect-claude-to-traverse.html', label: 'How do I connect Claude to Traverse?' },
  { href: '/questions/how-does-the-mcp-server-work.html', label: 'How does the Traverse MCP server work?' },
  { href: '/questions/how-do-ai-agents-discover-capabilities.html', label: 'How do AI agents discover capabilities?' },
];

const _jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'How do I connect Cursor to Traverse?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'You can point a Cursor MCP server entry at a locally built traverse-mcp binary with the stdio argument, but on v0.15.0 that alone will not give Cursor a tools list: traverse-mcp speaks its own line-delimited JSON command protocol (describe_server, list_entrypoints, execute_entrypoint and so on), not the standard MCP JSON-RPC tools/list and tools/call methods, so a generic MCP client needs a translation layer in front of it. Build from a traverse checkout; the crates.io 0.15.0 binary cannot start outside one (traverse#1720).',
    },
  }],
});

const _body = `<p>Cursor launches MCP servers as stdio subprocesses, and <code>traverse-mcp</code> is a stdio server, so the wiring is a config entry. The catch on v0.15.0: <code>traverse-mcp</code> speaks its own line-delimited JSON command protocol (one object per line with a <code>command</code> field such as <code>describe_server</code>, <code>list_entrypoints</code> or <code>execute_entrypoint</code>), not the standard MCP JSON-RPC <code>tools/list</code> / <code>tools/call</code> methods. Cursor won't show Traverse tools from this config alone; you need a small translation layer in front of the binary. Contract enforcement stays inside Traverse either way — Cursor is the IDE façade, not the authority for business rules.</p>

  <h2>Minimal Cursor config</h2>
  <p>After <code>cargo build -p traverse-mcp</code> in a traverse checkout, add something like this under <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "traverse": {
      "command": "/absolute/path/to/target/debug/traverse-mcp",
      "args": ["stdio"]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
  <p>The <code>stdio</code> argument is required. Relative paths break when Cursor's working directory is not the repo root — use an absolute path to the binary you just built.</p>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>
  <p>Build from a checkout for now: the crates.io 0.15.0 binary from <code>cargo install</code> can't start outside one (<a href="/questions/why-does-traverse-mcp-fail-with-catalog-load-failed.html">catalog_load_failed</a>, traverse#1720), and there is no prebuilt <code>traverse-mcp-v*</code> release yet. Treat shapes you see in docs as illustrative unless you have verified them against the running binary and <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/main/docs/mcp-stdio-server.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs/mcp-stdio-server.md</a>. Local MCP is a trust-the-host setup; optional bearer-token guidance lives in the core docs if you need a tighter boundary.</p>

  <h2>Related setup</h2>
  <p>Step-by-step copy for both Cursor and Claude Desktop is on the <a href="/docs/guides/mcp-setup.html">MCP setup guide</a>. If you are choosing between "agent proposes" and "runtime decides," see <a href="/questions/what-is-the-difference-between-an-ai-agent-and-the-traverse-runtime.html">agent vs runtime</a>.</p>`;
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="How do I connect Cursor to Traverse?"
  description="Point Cursor's MCP config at a local traverse-mcp binary with args including stdio. Same governed tool surface Claude Desktop uses — contracts stay on the Traverse side."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-do-i-connect-cursor-to-traverse.html"
  category="AI Agents and MCP"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
